PHASE 3 - OPTIONAL TRANSCRIPT RETENTION
=======================================
Written 2026-08-21 on the M3 (96 GB), implemented and verified there the same
day. Phases 0 to 2 are unchanged by this work apart from one move, noted under
"What moved". 165 tests pass.


What is new
-----------
Two conversation states, ephemeral and retained, and three commands that switch
between them:

  START_RETAINING     begin keeping this session's transcript
  STOP_RETAINING      stop keeping it; what is written stays written
  TOGGLE_RETENTION    alt-cmd-T, for a hotkey that cannot know the current state

STATUS reports which state the session is in and where the transcript is going.
RVW_RETENTION=retained makes a session start out keeping its transcript;
ephemeral is the default and writes nothing whatever.


Archive layout
--------------
  var/meetings/YYYY/MM/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM/transcript.jsonl
                                       /metadata.json
                                       /transcript.md
                                       /screenshots/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.SS.mmm.png
                                                   /YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.SS.mmm.json

The transcript lands in the directory the screenshots of the same session were
already going into, which is what Phase 2 left room for. That is the whole
association between an image and the speech around it: same directory, and both
timestamped. No index is involved and none can be lost.

transcript.jsonl, one object per utterance, appended and never rewritten:

  {"end_epoch": 1787376745.437, "speaker": "them", "start_epoch": 1787376741.537,
   "start_local": "2026-08-21T22:32:21", "stream": "system",
   "text": "The lease timeout was 30 seconds, and the client should reconnect."}

The speaker label and the local time are stored even though both are derivable,
because the value of a canonical file is that it can be read in five years
without this repository. metadata.json holds what the transcript cannot say about
itself: the session's start and end, the streams and their labels, the host, and
the recognition and LLM models that produced the text. transcript.md is rendered
from the JSONL, one utterance per paragraph, and says in its own first lines that
it is derived and will be rewritten.


Design decisions
----------------
Retention hangs off RollingTranscript.add, the one funnel every recognised
utterance already passes through. The transcript offers each utterance it
accepted to a sink and knows nothing else: whether anything is written is
entirely the archive's decision, and nothing in the recognition path knows about
files. The sink is called outside the transcript's lock, because it writes to a
disk and the capture threads must not queue behind that.

Ephemeral means nothing is written, not that something is written and deleted.
An ephemeral session creates no directory and no file, so there is nothing to
find, nothing to forget to delete, and nothing to explain to whoever was in the
conversation.

Switching retention on is not retrospective. The rolling window still holds up to
half an hour of speech at that moment, and writing it would be keeping words that
were said while the session was ephemeral. Switching retention off, conversely,
leaves what is already written: it is a decision about what happens next, not a
way to unsay anything. Both directions are logged.

The file is opened and closed per utterance rather than held open. Utterances
arrive every few seconds, so the cost is irrelevant, and a crash or a power cut
then leaves a complete file rather than a partial last line.

A line that cannot be read back is fatal. read_transcript_records refuses a
transcript with a line that is not JSON or that lacks the fields every record
has, naming the file and the line number, because a rendering silently one line
short would be believed. That is also why transcript.md is derived and never
read: a rendering can never disagree with the record it came from.

Nothing here is a database, which principle 7 of the specification requires. An
index over these files belongs in Phase 4 and has to be rebuildable from them.

An unwritable disk stops retention rather than reporting once per utterance. It
will not improve within the session, and one FAIL is more useful than hundreds.


What moved
----------
session_archive_dir moved from rvw/screenshot.py to rvw/meeting_archive.py, with
local_time_text beside it, and screenshot.py imports both. The meeting directory
is no longer a screenshot concept: it holds the transcript, the metadata and the
images, so it belongs to the module that owns the meeting. Nothing else changed
about screen capture.


Verified on the M3
------------------
- An ephemeral session, driven through the real control socket, left no file and
  no directory behind: STATUS said "ephemeral (the transcript is not being kept)"
  and var/meetings stayed empty.
- A retained session wrote the JSONL, the metadata and the rendering. Real speech
  played through the system audio tap and recognised by Whisper arrived in
  transcript.jsonl with its timestamps, and in transcript.md with its speaker.
- TOGGLE_RETENTION switched both ways through bin/rvwctl, and QUIT rendered the
  Markdown on the way out.
- A session that retained nothing still writes metadata.json and a transcript.md
  saying no speech was recorded, which is honest rather than an empty directory.


